# Pots from clay: pinch, coil and slab, coils counted against a height, slabs and joins counted from
# a pot's net, scored and slipped joins in order, the Jomon potters of Japan, and a pot made by
# hand.
lesson art-pots-from-clay v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=7 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Pots from clay: pinch, coil and slab"
  goal "Plan a clay pot by its method: how many coils, how many slabs, and how to join them."

  look {
    scene 36x10 {
      row all space=3 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        solid ball kind="sphere" label="pinch"
        solid jar kind="cylinder" label="coil"
        solid box kind="cuboid" label="slab"
      }
    }
    say """
      There are three ways to build a pot by hand. A pinch pot is pressed out of one ball of clay
      with the thumb. A coil pot is built from long ropes of clay laid round, one on top of another,
      and smoothed together. A slab pot is made from flat sheets cut out and joined. Where two
      pieces join, potters scratch both edges and brush on slip, which is clay stirred with water,
      so that the join holds. In Japan, the Jomon potters built pots from coils thousands of years
      ago and pressed cord into the clay; Jomon means rope-patterned.
      """
  }
  do {
    show art.clay-method k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show art.clay-coils t=1 h=8 hard-t=2 hard-h=10
    level easy {
      worked art.clay-slabs s=0 level=medium
    }
    level medium hard {
      show art.clay-slabs s=0 hard-s=1
    }
    show art.clay-joins s=0 hard-s=1
    show art.clay-order k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
    show art.clay-method k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show art.clay-time q=0 hard-q=2
    show art.clay-coils t=1 h=7 easy-h=6 hard-t=2 hard-h=6
    show art.clay-order k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.clay-why-score r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.clay-make v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Pinch from one ball, coil from ropes, slab from flat sheets; score and slip every join."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a pot is planned by how it will be built: a pinch pot is pressed from one ball,
    a coil pot rises one coil at a time, and a slab pot is a box of flat sheets whose every join is
    scored and slipped. The wrong ideas to watch for are counting only the joins round the base of a
    slab box, and forgetting that thicker coils mean fewer of them. In the working, look for the
    child touching each edge of the drawn net once as they count. The core sorts the three methods,
    counts coils against a height, slabs and joins from a net, puts the steps of a join in order and
    counts years back to the Jomon potters; the two-star asks why the edges are scratched, and the
    three-star is a pot made by hand, which you respond to rather than mark.

    The facts are from Wikipedia: coil building ("Coiling (pottery)", which dates Japanese
    coil-built pottery to about 10,500 BC in the Jomon period), scoring and slip ("Slip
    (ceramics)"), and Jomon pottery ("Jomon pottery": rope-patterned, fired at about 600 to 900
    degrees, a flame-rimmed pot of 3000 to 2000 BC in the Tokyo National Museum). Air-drying clay or
    salt dough works for the pot; it does not need a kiln. If the joins are stuck, build the box
    from six squares of card and count the edges as they are taped. Off the page, look at the Tokyo
    National Museum's Jomon pots online. This lesson meets Japan's 2017 course of study for art and
    craft, grades 3 and 4, A表現 (1)イ:
    「絵や立体，工作に表す活動を通して…表したいことや用途などを考え，形や色，材料などを生かしながら，どのように表すかについて考えること」 (in pictures,
    three-dimensional work and craft, think about what to make and its use, and how to make it), and
    its note that every grade should have 「版に表す経験や焼成する経験」 (the experience of printing and of
    firing).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the methods are read from what the potter does, the coils are 1 cm thick, counting slabs is worked once in the teacher's pen before joins of an open box, the steps of a join are in a gentler order, the years counted are a thousand, one more method is sorted, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the pot is a pinch pot."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the methods are read from what the pot looks like, the coils are 2 cm thick, the slabs and joins are for a box with a lid, the years are counted to 1000 BC and to the end of the Jomon period, and the pot is a tall coil pot on a slab base."
  }
}
